from expand_common import *
f=family
t='d_relations'
f(t,'equivalence-size','A relation built from equal classes',r'A set of $n^2$ elements is partitioned into $n$ classes of size $n$. Count ordered pairs in the induced equivalence relation.',r'$n^3$',[r'$n^2$',r'$n^4$',r'$n(n-1)^2$'],lambda n:n**3,[lambda n:n*n,lambda n:n**4,lambda n:n*(n-1)**2],r'Each class contributes every ordered pair of its members, including diagonal pairs. Its contribution is $n^2$, and the disjoint classes contribute independently to the relation size. Sum the class contributions rather than counting unordered edges.','For unequal classes, sum the squares of their sizes.',['Every equivalence relation has all classes equally large.','Only unordered distinct pairs belong to an equivalence relation.','The relation size equals the number of classes.'],'Reflexivity includes diagonals and symmetry includes both orientations. An equivalence class contributes its full Cartesian square even when its size differs from the others. Equal class sizes are a premise of this calculation, not a property of equivalence relations.')
f(t,'symmetric-diagonal','Reflexive symmetric Boolean matrices',r'Count reflexive symmetric relations on an $n$-element labeled set.',r'$2^{n(n-1)/2}$',[r'$2^{n^2}$',r'$2^{n(n-1)}$',r'$2^n$'],lambda n:2**C(n,2),[lambda n:2**(n*n),lambda n:2**(n*(n-1)),lambda n:2**n],r'Reflexivity fixes all diagonal entries to one. Each unordered distinct pair has one free bit because its two directed entries must agree. Thus only the upper-triangular off-diagonal positions are independently chosen.','Reflexive symmetry does not imply transitivity.',['Every symmetric reflexive relation is an equivalence relation.','Symmetry forces all diagonal entries to zero.','Each directed pair can be chosen independently under symmetry.'],'Take three elements with the two adjacent undirected links present and the third missing, plus all loops. Symmetry and reflexivity hold, but a two-edge path violates transitivity. Counting only equivalence relations requires an additional partition constraint.')
f(t,'antisymmetric','Antisymmetry without an imposed diagonal',r'Count antisymmetric relations on an $n$-element labeled set, with diagonal pairs optional.',r'$2^n3^{n(n-1)/2}$',[r'$3^{n(n-1)/2}$',r'$2^{n^2}$',r'$2^{n(n-1)/2}$'],lambda n:2**n*3**C(n,2),[lambda n:3**C(n,2),lambda n:2**(n*n),lambda n:2**C(n,2)],r'For a distinct unordered pair, choose no directed edge, its first orientation, or its second orientation. Both orientations together are forbidden. Diagonal pairs are unrestricted by antisymmetry, contributing another $2^n$ factor.','Antisymmetry allows loops and missing pairs.',['Antisymmetry is the negation of symmetry.','An antisymmetric relation cannot contain loops.','Every antisymmetric relation compares every distinct pair.'],'Antisymmetry only restricts bidirectional edges between distinct elements. It does not impose reflexivity, total comparability, or transitivity. The identity relation is both symmetric and antisymmetric, which also refutes treating the properties as logical negations.')
f(t,'strict-symmetry','Symmetric irreflexive relations',r'Count symmetric irreflexive relations on an $n$-element labeled set.',r'$2^{n(n-1)/2}$',[r'$2^{n(n-1)}$',r'$2^n$',r'$2^{n^2}$'],lambda n:2**C(n,2),[lambda n:2**(n*(n-1)),lambda n:2**n,lambda n:2**(n*n)],r'Irreflexivity fixes the diagonal to zero. Symmetry couples the two entries of each distinct unordered pair, leaving one independent decision per pair. This is exactly the count of simple undirected graphs on labeled vertices.','A nonempty symmetric irreflexive relation cannot also be transitive.',['Every undirected graph relation is transitive.','Transitivity removes the symmetry requirement.','Irreflexivity allows arbitrary diagonal bits.'],'Any symmetric edge from one distinct element to another and back forms a path of length two. Transitivity would require a loop at its start, contradicting irreflexivity. The only relation satisfying all three properties is therefore the empty relation.')
f(t,'residue-classes','An equivalence generated by congruence',r'On integers $0,\ldots,2n-1$, relate two numbers when their difference is divisible by $n$. Count ordered related pairs.',r'$4n$',[r'$2n$',r'$n^2$',r'$4n^2$'],lambda n:4*n,[lambda n:2*n,lambda n:n*n,lambda n:4*n*n],r'The $n$ residue classes each contain two elements. Each class contributes four ordered pairs, including its two loops. Add these contributions over all residue classes; no pairs from different classes are related.','Congruence classes are determined by the modulus, not by numeric proximity.',['Each class contains only one element of this interval.','Distinct numbers can never be equivalent.','The difference must equal the modulus rather than be divisible by it.'],'The relation includes zero differences and both positive and negative multiples of the modulus. In this finite interval each residue occurs twice, so two distinct representatives can belong to the same equivalence class.')
f(t,'order-size','Reflexive versus strict chains',r'For the ordinary non-strict order on $\{1,\ldots,n\}$, count ordered related pairs.',r'$n(n+1)/2$',[r'$n(n-1)/2$',r'$n^2$',r'$n$'],lambda n:n*(n+1)//2,[lambda n:n*(n-1)//2,lambda n:n*n,lambda n:n],r'For each first argument, count itself and the larger arguments. The row lengths are $n,n-1$ down to one. Equivalently take one orientation for each distinct pair and add the diagonal.','Deleting the diagonal changes a non-strict order into its associated strict order.',['A strict order must be reflexive.','Every partial order compares all distinct elements.','The non-strict order contains both orientations of each distinct pair.'],'A strict order is irreflexive and transitive; a non-strict order includes equality. Total comparability holds for this particular numeric chain but is not required for a general partial order, whose elements may be incomparable.')
f(t,'closure-chain','Reachability in a directed chain',r'A directed path on $n$ vertices has edges only from each vertex to the next. Count pairs in its transitive closure without adding identity pairs.',r'$n(n-1)/2$',[r'$n(n+1)/2$',r'$n-1$',r'$n^2$'],lambda n:n*(n-1)//2,[lambda n:n*(n+1)//2,lambda n:n-1,lambda n:n*n],r'Reachability holds from any earlier vertex to any later vertex. It does not hold backward or to itself because this path has no cycle and only positive-length paths are included. Count all distinct ordered forward pairs.','Reflexive-transitive closure additionally includes zero-length paths.',['Transitive closure always contains identity pairs by definition.','Reachability in a directed path is symmetric.','Adding shortcut edges creates backward reachability.'],'A positive-length transitive closure and a reflexive-transitive closure use different path-length conventions. Zero-length paths contribute exactly the diagonal. A directed cycle, unlike this acyclic path, can create diagonal reachability even under the positive-length convention.')
f(t,'transitive-triplets','How many closure tests must be understood',r'An $n$-element domain is used to test transitivity by examining all ordered triples $(x,y,z)$, allowing repeated elements. Count candidate triples.',r'$n^3$',[r'$\binom n3$',r'$n(n-1)(n-2)$',r'$n^2$'],lambda n:n**3,[lambda n:C(n,3),lambda n:n*(n-1)*(n-2),lambda n:n*n],r'The definition quantifies independently over three ordered arguments. Repetition is permitted, so each position has $n$ choices. A triple is a potential implication test; only triples with both premise edges actually demand the conclusion edge.','Repeated-vertex tests can force loops when cycles occur.',['Only three distinct vertices matter in every transitivity test.','Every missing edge independently violates transitivity.','Transitivity requires every ordered triple to have all three edges.'],'The implication is triggered only by two present premise edges. Choosing a start equal to the endpoint detects loops forced by a two-way connection. Ignoring repeated vertices therefore loses part of the definition, particularly for symmetric relations.')

t='d_functions'
f(t,'injection','A fixed small domain with a growing codomain',r'Count injective functions from a three-element labeled domain into an $(n+3)$-element codomain.',r'$(n+3)(n+2)(n+1)$',[r'$(n+3)^3$',r'$\binom{n+3}{3}$',r'$3^{n+3}$'],lambda n:(n+3)*(n+2)*(n+1),[lambda n:(n+3)**3,lambda n:C(n+3,3),lambda n:3**(n+3)],r'Choose the first image, then a different second image, then a third different image. Ordered domain elements distinguish assignments to the same selected three-element image set, so a binomial coefficient alone omits a permutation factor.','Injectivity is impossible if the domain is larger than the codomain.',['A function may leave some domain arguments undefined.','Selecting an image set alone specifies an injective function.','Finite injectivity always implies surjectivity even for unequal sizes.'],'A total function assigns every domain element an image. The pigeonhole principle blocks injections into a smaller finite codomain. Injectivity implies surjectivity only when the two finite sizes agree; this example deliberately has extra available targets.')
f(t,'onto-three','Surjectivity and overlapping omissions',r'Count surjections from an $n$-element labeled set onto a three-element labeled set.',r'$3^n-3\cdot2^n+3$',[r'$3^n$',r'$3^n-3\cdot2^n$',r'$3!\binom n3$'],lambda n:3**n-3*2**n+3,[lambda n:3**n,lambda n:3**n-3*2**n,lambda n:6*C(n,3)],r'Begin with all functions. Subtract the three events omitting a specified target, each with $2^n$ functions. Restore the three pairwise intersections, each a constant function; the triple intersection is empty for a nonempty domain.','Restoring overlaps is essential because constant maps omit two targets.',['The omitted-target events are disjoint.','Choosing three preimages determines every surjection uniquely.','A nonempty domain can map into an empty available target set.'],'The constant maps are subtracted twice in the first subtraction and need one restoration. Counting selected representatives of the three fibers instead overcounts functions with multiple possible representatives, so a witness-selection count is not a function count.')
f(t,'idempotent-two','A fixed image under an idempotent map',r'Count functions on an $n$-element set with image size two and $f\circ f=f$.',r'$\binom n2\,2^{n-2}$',[r'$\binom n2\,2^n$',r'$2^n$',r'$n(n-1)$'],lambda n:C(n,2)*2**(n-2),[lambda n:C(n,2)*2**n,lambda n:2**n,lambda n:n*(n-1)],r'Choose the two image points. Idempotence forces each image point to be fixed. Every other element independently maps to one of these points. The fixed image points ensure that both targets occur, so no additional onto correction is needed.','Idempotence means every image point is fixed, not every domain point.',['Idempotence requires every domain point to be fixed.','Every function with two image points is idempotent.','An image point may map to the other image point in an idempotent map.'],'Apply the equation to an arbitrary preimage of an image point; the image point must map to itself. Conversely fixing the image makes every second application agree with the first. The remaining domain points may collapse onto either fixed image point.')
f(t,'involution-pairs','Two exchanged pairs and all other points fixed',r'Count permutations on an $n$-element set having exactly two disjoint transpositions and no other nontrivial cycles.',r'$3\binom n4$',[r'$6\binom n4$',r'$\binom n2^2$',r'$\binom n4$'],lambda n:3*C(n,4),[lambda n:6*C(n,4),lambda n:C(n,2)**2,lambda n:C(n,4)],r'Choose the four moved points. They admit three partitions into two unordered pairs: pair one selected point with each of the three others, and the last pair is forced. The transpositions commute and must not be ordered again.','An involution may contain fixed points and disjoint transpositions, but no longer cycles.',['A four-cycle is an involution.','The two disjoint transpositions must be ordered in the count.','An involution cannot have fixed points.'],'Squaring a cycle returns every point only when its length divides two. Thus the cycle decomposition of an involution has lengths one or two. Ordering disjoint transpositions represents the same permutation twice and introduces a spurious factor.')
f(t,'monotone-three','Nondecreasing functions into a short chain',r'Count weakly increasing functions from $\{1,\ldots,n\}$ to $\{1,2,3\}$.',r'$\binom{n+2}{2}$',[r'$3^n$',r'$\binom n3$',r'$n^3$'],lambda n:C(n+2,2),[lambda n:3**n,lambda n:C(n,3),lambda n:n**3],r'An increasing output sequence is fixed by the counts of its three values. Those counts are nonnegative and sum to $n$. Stars and bars gives two separators among $n+2$ positions, with no additional rearrangement factor.','Strict increase would prohibit repeated output values.',['Weak increase requires all three outputs to occur.','Permuting an increasing sequence always preserves it.','Monotone maps and arbitrary maps have the same count.'],'Weak increase allows repeated values and omitted values. Strict increase requires distinct ordered outputs, so it is impossible when the domain has more than three points. The distinction changes the admissible multiplicity vectors completely.')
f(t,'right-inverse','Choosing one representative from every fiber',r'A surjection has three labeled target points, each with exactly $n$ preimages. Count right inverses $g$ satisfying $f\circ g=\operatorname{id}$.',r'$n^3$',[r'$3^n$',r'$(3n)^3$',r'$n!$'],lambda n:n**3,[lambda n:3**n,lambda n:(3*n)**3,lambda n:math.factorial(n)],r'For each target choose one preimage in its own fiber. The fibers are disjoint and the three choices are independent. Choosing from the whole domain instead of the correct fiber would generally violate the right-inverse equation.','A right inverse implies surjectivity and is injective itself.',['A right inverse implies that the original function is injective.','Two different targets may have the same chosen preimage.','A missing fiber can still supply a right inverse.'],'The equation supplies a preimage of each target, proving onto-ness. If two targets had the same representative, applying the original function would make them equal, so the representative selection is injective even though the original map need not be.')
f(t,'one-cycle','Cyclic permutations with no distinguished starting point',r'Count permutations of an $n$-element labeled set consisting of one cycle.',r'$(n-1)!$',[r'$n!$',r'$2^n$',r'$n(n-1)$'],lambda n:math.factorial(n-1),[lambda n:math.factorial(n),lambda n:2**n,lambda n:n*(n-1)],r'Write a cycle with one specified label first. Arrange the remaining labels in the subsequent positions. Rotations of a cycle describe the same permutation; fixing its first displayed label removes exactly that representational redundancy.','Reversing a cycle usually changes the permutation.',['Every cycle and its reverse define the same permutation.','Rotations of the displayed cycle are different functions.','A single cycle fixes every point.'],'Rotation changes only the starting location in the notation, whereas reversal changes which point maps to which successor. For cycles of length greater than two, the reverse is generally the inverse permutation rather than the same permutation.')
f(t,'fixed-points','Specified fixed points versus exactly two fixed points',r'Count permutations on an $n$-element set that fix two specified distinct points; other fixed points are allowed.',r'$(n-2)!$',[r'$(n-1)!$',r'$n!$',r'$\binom n2$'],lambda n:math.factorial(n-2),[lambda n:math.factorial(n-1),lambda n:math.factorial(n),lambda n:C(n,2)],r'The two specified images are forced. The remaining points must permute among themselves, giving the factorial of their number. No derangement restriction is imposed on the remaining permutation because additional fixed points are explicitly allowed.','Exactly two fixed points would require a derangement on the remaining points.',['Fixing two specified points means no other point may be fixed.','The two specified points can exchange their images.','Choosing the fixed points completes the permutation.'],'For exactly two fixed points, select their identities and forbid all other fixed points. That is a different problem involving a derangement factor. Specified versus unspecified identities and at-least versus exactly conditions must both be preserved.')

t='d_invariants'
f(t,'weighted-potential','A transfer invariant with a weighted component',r'Start at $a=n,b=n+1$. Repeat $(a,b)\leftarrow(a-1,b+2)$ until $a=0$. Find the final $b$.',r'$3n+1$',[r'$2n+1$',r'$n+1$',r'$3n$'],lambda n:3*n+1,[lambda n:2*n+1,lambda n:n+1,lambda n:3*n],r'The weighted total $2a+b$ is preserved: the first coordinate loses one while the second gains two. Initially it equals $3n+1$. At the exit the first coordinate is zero, so the invariant fixes the second coordinate without simulating each transfer.','A preserved sum proves the exit value only after termination and the exit condition are established.',['Preservation alone proves termination.','The invariant must strictly decrease.','A final value follows without using the exit condition.'],'The first component is a nonnegative decreasing ranking, proving termination. The weighted total is the separate invariant. Confusing a preserved expression with a decreasing ranking loses either the correctness argument or the termination argument.')
f(t,'gcd-euclid','The invariant of simultaneous remainder updates',r'Run the Euclidean remainder algorithm from $a=30n,b=18n$. Find the final nonzero value.',r'$6n$',[r'$12n$',r'$18n$',r'$30n$'],lambda n:6*n,[lambda n:12*n,lambda n:18*n,lambda n:30*n],r'The set of common divisors is unchanged by replacing the first value by the remainder after subtracting a multiple of the second. The terminal pair has the gcd as its nonzero entry. Factor out $n$ and compute the gcd of 30 and 18.','A sequential overwrite can destroy the intended simultaneous state transition.',['Using the new first value in the remainder is always equivalent.','The gcd must strictly decrease on every step.','The first coordinate is always the ranking function.'],'A temporary preserves the old values needed by the simultaneous pair update. The gcd is invariant, while the nonnegative second coordinate strictly decreases. The algorithm is correct only if the implementation performs the stated transition with the old operands.')
f(t,'two-counter-ranking','Nested progress measured by one potential',r'Start at $x=n,y=n$. If $x>0$, replace $x$ by $x-1$ and add $n$ to $y$; otherwise decrease positive $y$ by one. Stop at $(0,0)$. Count transitions.',r'$n^2+2n$',[r'$n^2$',r'$n^2+n$',r'$3n$'],lambda n:n*n+2*n,[lambda n:n*n,lambda n:n*n+n,lambda n:3*n],r'The integer potential $(n+1)x+y$ drops by exactly one in either branch. It begins at $n^2+2n$ and ends at zero, so it counts transitions exactly. The raw sum of coordinates can increase and is not a valid decreasing ranking.','A ranking may be weighted even when one state component grows.',['Every state component must decrease separately.','A bounded invariant alone counts all transitions.','A potential may decrease below zero without affecting termination.'],'A well-founded ranking is nonnegative and strictly decreases along every permitted transition. It may combine several coordinates to offset temporary growth in one. An invariant, by contrast, stays constant and supplies no step count on its own.')
f(t,'mixed-branching','Leaf accounting under two constructor arities',r'A finite rooted tree has $n$ binary internal nodes and $n$ ternary internal nodes. Find its leaf count.',r'$3n+1$',[r'$2n+1$',r'$4n+1$',r'$5n$'],lambda n:3*n+1,[lambda n:2*n+1,lambda n:4*n+1,lambda n:5*n],r'The internal nodes contribute $2n+3n$ edges. Total nodes are the $2n$ internal nodes plus the leaves. Equate edges with nodes minus one and solve for the leaf count. The calculation does not require any balance assumption.','The degree-count identity depends on the object being a tree.',['A directed graph with cycles has the same node-edge equation.','Only height controls leaf count.','Binary and ternary nodes contribute the same edge count.'],'A tree has a unique parent for every nonroot node and no extra cycle edges. Those properties establish the edge count used by the invariant. A general graph can have additional edges, so the same degree totals do not determine its leaf count.')
f(t,'accumulator-checkpoint','The exact checkpoint for an arithmetic accumulator',r'Initialize $s=0,i=2$. Repeat $s\leftarrow s+i$ and then $i\leftarrow i+1$, exactly $n$ times. Find $s$.',r'$n(n+3)/2$',[r'$n(n+1)/2$',r'$n(n+2)/2$',r'$n^2$'],lambda n:Fraction(n*(n+3),2),[lambda n:Fraction(n*(n+1),2),lambda n:Fraction(n*(n+2),2),lambda n:n*n],r'At the start of iteration $j$, the next term is $j+1$. The processed terms after $n$ iterations are 2 through $n+1$. Their sum is the ordinary prefix sum through $n+1$ minus its initial one.','Moving the increment before the addition shifts every accumulated term.',['The order of updates never affects the invariant.','The invariant uses the same index at every checkpoint.','Every increasing accumulator is a termination ranking.'],'The invariant must name whether it holds before or after the update. Incrementing first changes the summed sequence to 3 through $n+2$. This implementation change preserves termination but changes both the invariant equation and the returned value.')
f(t,'recursive-state-count','Counting every node of a recursion tree',r'Every nonleaf recursive call makes three calls. All leaves have depth $n$, with the root at depth zero. Count calls including root and leaves.',r'$(3^{n+1}-1)/2$',[r'$3^n$',r'$3^{n+1}$',r'$(3^n-1)/2$'],lambda n:(3**(n+1)-1)//2,[lambda n:3**n,lambda n:3**(n+1),lambda n:(3**n-1)//2],r'At depth $j$, induction on the constructor gives exactly $3^j$ calls. Sum these level counts from zero through the leaf depth. The geometric-series endpoint is one level beyond the last exponent, and the root contributes one.','A recursion depth is not a count of all calls.',['Three branches imply only three total calls at every depth.','Counting leaves always counts the root as well.','Maximum stack depth equals the total number of tree nodes.'],'A depth-first execution retains only a path of active calls, while the total work visits many paths. The all-calls count includes internal nodes and leaves; confusing depth, leaf count and total nodes yields the three competing expressions.')
f(t,'periodic-projection','A residue invariant far from the initial indices',r'A sequence of parities repeats odd, odd, even, beginning at index one. Count odd terms among indices one through $3n-1$.',r'$2n$',[r'$2n-1$',r'$n$',r'$3n-1$'],lambda n:2*n,[lambda n:2*n-1,lambda n:n,lambda n:3*n-1],r'The first $n-1$ complete blocks contribute two odds each. The remaining two positions are both odd. Thus the total is $2(n-1)+2$. Count the incomplete final block separately rather than applying a proportion to the entire prefix.','A finite-state cycle can answer a residue question without constructing large integer terms.',['Equal numbers of odds and evens must occur in every recurrence.','The incomplete final block can always be discarded.','Matching a few residues proves a cycle without checking its transition rule.'],'The transition of a state must establish the repeating pattern; once that is proven, integer magnitudes are irrelevant to the parity count. The endpoint determines how many terms of the final period are included and can change an exact answer.')
f(t,'balanced-resource','A conserved difference in a doubling transition',r'Start with $a=n,b=1$. Apply the simultaneous transition $(a,b)\leftarrow(2a+1,2b)$ once. Compute $a-b$ afterward.',r'$2n-1$',[r'$n-1$',r'$2n+1$',r'$2n$'],lambda n:2*n-1,[lambda n:n-1,lambda n:2*n+1,lambda n:2*n],r'Subtract the new coordinates: the difference transforms as $d\mapsto2d+1$. The shifted difference $d+1$ doubles. Initially $d=n-1$, so after one transition it is $2n-1$; preserving the affine offset is essential.','A transformed potential can simplify an affine state transition.',['Every useful invariant is a raw sum of coordinates.','The difference is unchanged by this transition.','An affine constant can always be dropped in exact arithmetic.'],'The raw difference is not conserved here, so calling it an invariant would be false. The relation between its value and the number of transitions is the useful state property. Shifting an affine recurrence can expose a multiplicative structure without discarding its constant.')

t='d_number'
f(t,'linear-congruence','Multiplicity of solutions of a congruence',r'Count residue classes $x$ modulo $6n$ solving $6x\equiv12\pmod{6n}$.',r'$6$',[r'$n$',r'$6n$',r'$3n$'],lambda n:6,[lambda n:n,lambda n:6*n,lambda n:3*n],r'The gcd of the coefficient and modulus is six, and it divides the right-hand side. Divide both sides and the modulus by six to obtain $x\equiv2\pmod n$. Its six lifts in the original modulus are distinct solutions.','Division reduces the modulus; retaining the original modulus would lose solutions.',['A coefficient may be canceled without changing the modulus.','Every linear congruence has exactly one solution.','Solvability does not depend on the right-hand side.'],'A congruence with coefficient gcd $d$ is solvable only when that gcd divides the right-hand side; then it has exactly $d$ solution classes modulo the original modulus. The reduced equation describes the common pattern of these lifts.')
f(t,'crt-adjacent','An explicit Chinese-remainder representative',r'Assume $n\ge2$. Find the least nonnegative $x$ with $x\equiv1\pmod n$ and $x\equiv2\pmod{n+1}$.',r'$n^2+1$',[r'$n^2$',r'$n(n+1)-1$',r'$n+1$'],lambda n:n*n+1,[lambda n:n*n,lambda n:n*(n+1)-1,lambda n:n+1],r'Set $x=1+nt$. Modulo $n+1$, the coefficient $n$ is minus one, so $1-t\equiv2$ and $t\equiv n$. Taking this representative gives $n^2+1$, below the product modulus. Coprime adjacent moduli ensure uniqueness.','For noncoprime moduli, compatible residues and a least-common-multiple period replace this simple product rule.',['Every pair of congruences is compatible.','Noncoprime moduli always forbid solutions.','The product is always the smallest solution period.'],'Common factors impose compatibility of the two residues modulo their gcd. When compatible, the joint period is the lcm, not necessarily the product. Adjacent positive moduli are coprime, which is why the product rule is valid here.')
f(t,'factorial-two','Prime valuation of a factorial',r'Find the largest exponent of 2 dividing $n!$.',r'$n-s_2(n)$',[r'$n$',r'$\lfloor n/2\rfloor$',r'$n-1$'],lambda n:n-n.bit_count(),[lambda n:n,lambda n:n//2,lambda n:n-1],r'Count multiples of 2, then the additional factors in multiples of 4, 8 and so forth. Writing $n$ in binary and summing these floors gives $n-s_2(n)$, where $s_2$ is the number of one-bits. Even numbers can contribute more than one factor.','Counting only even factors misses higher prime powers.',['Every even number contributes exactly one factor of two.','The valuation equals the numeric value of the factorial.','A valuation is independent of the prime being counted.'],'A multiple of four contributes at least two factors of two; subsequent floor terms account for these extras. The valuation method tracks prime exponents additively and is unrelated to the number of decimal digits or the magnitude of the factorial.',seeds=(6,10))
f(t,'divisor-product','Counting divisors from exponents',r'Find the number of positive divisors of $2^n3^2$.',r'$3(n+1)$',[r'$2n+1$',r'$2(n+1)$',r'$(n+1)^2$'],lambda n:3*(n+1),[lambda n:2*n+1,lambda n:2*(n+1),lambda n:(n+1)**2],r'A divisor chooses the exponent of 2 between zero and $n$ and the exponent of 3 between zero and two. Unique prime factorization makes these choices independent and ensures that different exponent pairs give different divisors.','The product rule needs distinct prime bases.',['Repeated appearances of the same prime are independent factors.','Only positive exponents are permitted in a divisor.','The divisor count is the sum of allowed exponent counts.'],'If two factors use the same prime, first combine their exponents; treating them independently counts repeated divisors. Zero exponents are allowed because a divisor may omit a prime entirely, including the divisor one.')
f(t,'square-divisors','A parity restriction on divisor exponents',r'Count square divisors of $2^{2n}3^{2n+1}5^2$.',r'$2(n+1)^2$',[r'$3(2n+1)(2n+2)$',r'$(n+1)^2$',r'$2n+2$'],lambda n:2*(n+1)**2,[lambda n:3*(2*n+1)*(2*n+2),lambda n:(n+1)**2,lambda n:2*n+2],r'A square divisor uses even exponents. There are $n+1$ even exponents for 2, $n+1$ for 3, and two for 5. Multiply these counts. The odd upper exponent of 3 does not create an additional even choice.','A perfect-square number requires every prime exponent to be even.',['Having an even total of prime exponents is sufficient.','A square divisor must include every prime of the original number.','Odd exponent bounds imply that no square divisor exists.'],'The square condition applies to every individual prime exponent, not their sum. A divisor can choose exponent zero for any prime. Even when an upper bound is odd, many even exponents below it remain available.')
f(t,'totient','Counting units rather than nonzero residues',r'Find the number of invertible residue classes modulo $2^n3^3$.',r'$18\cdot2^{n-1}$',[r'$27\cdot2^n$',r'$18\cdot2^n$',r'$9\cdot2^{n-1}$'],lambda n:18*2**(n-1),[lambda n:27*2**n,lambda n:18*2**n,lambda n:9*2**(n-1)],r'A unit is not divisible by 2 or 3. Apply inclusion-exclusion to the full residue system, or multiply the prime-power totients for the coprime factors. The resulting fraction of all residues is one half times two thirds.','Being nonzero modulo a composite modulus does not ensure invertibility.',['Every nonzero residue has an inverse.','Euler exponent reduction is valid for every base.','The unit count equals the modulus.'],'An inverse exists precisely when the residue is coprime to the modulus. For example, 2 is nonzero but not invertible under this modulus. Euler reduction requires this unit hypothesis, so a nonunit base cannot be reduced indiscriminately.')
f(t,'power-cycle','Reducing exponents by the actual residue cycle',r'Find the last decimal digit of $3^{4n+1}$.',r'$3$',[r'$1$',r'$7$',r'$9$'],lambda n:3,[lambda n:1,lambda n:7,lambda n:9],r'The successive last digits of powers of 3 are 3,9,7,1, repeating with period four. The exponent is congruent to one modulo that period. The modulus here is ten; reducing the exponent modulo ten would be an unrelated operation.','The cycle begins at exponent one and has period four.',['The exponent should always be reduced modulo the output modulus.','Every base has the same period modulo ten.','The last digit determines the full integer power.'],'The periodicity follows from repeated modular multiplication. Since 3 is a unit modulo ten, its powers form a cycle from the start; different bases can have different periods or a transient part, so the actual cycle must be identified.')
f(t,'gcd-prime-vectors','Taking componentwise minima',r'Find $\gcd(2^n3^{n+2},2^{n+1}3^n)$.',r'$6^n$',[r'$2\cdot6^n$',r'$9\cdot6^n$',r'$18^n$'],lambda n:6**n,[lambda n:2*6**n,lambda n:9*6**n,lambda n:18**n],r'A common divisor cannot use a prime exponent larger than either operand allows. Taking the smaller exponent for each prime gives a common divisor containing every possible common prime power. Both minima are $n$.','The lcm takes maxima, while the gcd takes minima.',['The gcd adds prime exponents.','The lcm takes the smaller exponent.','The gcd is always one when the operands differ.'],'The divisibility order corresponds to coordinatewise ordering of prime exponents. Minima describe the largest common divisor and maxima describe the least common multiple. Their coordinatewise sums equal the exponents of the product of the operands.')
